<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD PolicyKit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/PolicyKit/1/policyconfig.dtd">

<!--
  APEX agent runtime — the two passwords behind dimension 3.

  ROADMAP.md section 3.3 says root is delegated, not inherited: a managed agent
  does not become root because the user ran sudo an hour ago, because the agent
  is in native bypass mode, because the sandbox is unrestricted, or because
  Remote Control is driving it. Section 3.4 and section 7 both then say what it
  does take, and they say the same thing: local authentication.

  These are the two actions that ask. apex-agentd runs pkcheck against the
  process on the far end of its control socket, with user interaction allowed,
  and mints a grant only when that exits 0. The command line lives in
  apexd/apex-agent-core/src/auth.rs and not in this comment, because an XML
  comment may not contain two consecutive hyphens and every flag it passes
  begins with two. A polkit action file that is not well formed is not
  reported as an error: polkitd simply never registers the action, pkcheck
  exits 127 forever, and nothing else says why. That is exactly how P0-016's
  first attempt shipped, so this file is validated with xmllint at image build
  time.

  == why the subject is the caller and not the daemon =====================

  polkit dispatches an authentication challenge to the agent registered for
  the SUBJECT's login session. apex-agentd passes the peer of the control
  connection, which it has already established is not inside any managed
  session, so the dialog appears wherever that human is sitting. Passing the
  daemon, or the agent's own process, would put the prompt somewhere the agent
  could reach; section 4.4's "the user authenticates outside the agent PTY" is
  this choice of subject and nothing else.

  == auth_admin, not auth_self ===========================================

  P0-016's Always Unrestricted toggle uses auth_self, correctly: it moves the
  sandbox and grants no root at all. These two do grant root. A session grant
  pre-approves named privileged operations; break-glass clears no_new_privs,
  so setuid binaries work and the session can genuinely become root. Asking
  for administrator authentication is asking the question that is actually
  being answered.

  == and never the _keep variants ========================================

  auth_admin_keep caches the authorization for the rest of the login session,
  which is a window in which the answer is yes and nobody was asked. The whole
  value here is that every grant and every renewal is a fresh prompt: an agent
  that cannot renew its own grant gains nothing if a cached yes renews it
  instead. auth.rs states the same rule in code, and a change to one without
  the other is a bug in whichever was not changed.

  == allow_inactive and allow_any are no =================================

  A switched-away session and a remote one are both "nobody is at this
  keyboard". Section 7 reserves elevation for a local origin, and there is no
  password that makes a remote caller local, so they are refused outright
  rather than challenged. apex-agentd refuses them before it reaches polkit,
  from the connection's cgroup rather than from anything the caller claims;
  this is the same rule stated where an administrator can read it.

  Install (regular systemd host):
    sudo install -Dm644 files/system/polkit-1/actions/org.apexos.agent.policy \
         /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/org.apexos.agent.policy

  APEX-OS ships it read-only at that path from Containerfile.base.
-->

<policyconfig>

  <vendor>APEX-OS</vendor>
  <vendor_url>https://github.com/AndreNijman/apex-os</vendor_url>

  <!-- Section 4.4: a session-bound, capability-scoped, time-limited grant.
       The session stays under no_new_privs and keeps the secret broker; what
       it gains is that named privileged operations it files stop needing a
       separate decision until the grant runs out. -->
  <action id="org.apexos.agent.system-access">
    <description>Grant an agent session scoped system access</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to let this agent session perform named privileged operations without asking again.</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>no</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>no</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin</allow_active>
    </defaults>
  </action>

  <!-- Section 4.5: break-glass. This one clears no_new_privs, so the session
       can use sudo and become root. Deliberately a separate action from the
       one above: allowing the smaller thing must not allow this. -->
  <action id="org.apexos.agent.break-glass">
    <description>Run an agent session with the APEX protections removed</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to remove the APEX root boundary for this agent session. It will be able to become root until the time limit runs out.</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>no</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>no</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin</allow_active>
    </defaults>
  </action>

</policyconfig>
